nd. \"Self-preservation, self-reproduction, and energy conversion, something like that... But, for example, think about a cute wooden doll that doesn't fall over.\" [257] \"A... doll that doesn't fall over?\" Hiramatsu repeated. Repeating the other person's words indicates a long reaction time or, in most cases, a pause in thinking. [258] \"Let me explain. It's an organism because it's made of wood and has self-preservation, standing up again right after being pushed over... and it can convert potential energy into kinetic energy.\" [259] \"But it can't reproduce itself,\" Mizuki said. [260] \"The wooden doll is very cute, and people want to buy it as soon as they see it, so the manufacturer keeps producing it. In other words, it reproduces itself through its own cuteness.\" [261] \"But it's people who produce them, not themselves reproducing.\" [262] \"Many organisms in the world cannot reproduce without the help of other organisms,\" said Saito. \"For example, a beautiful flower is a signal to insects, asking them to help with self-reproduction.\" [263] \"So you think the toppling doll is a living thing?\" asked Momiji. [264] \"According to the definition just now, yes,\" answered Saito. \"So it's vague. Of course, the answer would be different if we defined it strictly using DNA and so on... so I think computer viruses are living things.\" [265] \"Well...,\" pouted Momiji. \"I can't understand it.\" [266] \"Let's go back to my room and eat the sweets that someone brought for us,\" said Akutagawa. [267] 6\n[268] The gift brought by Yoshitomi from the world is a round cake from Yokohama, not the cheap thing Momiji imagined. But Akutagawa was not happy when he saw the sweets. [269] In the next room, two male graduate students were discussing a problem with Professor Kozuka, who left the room after taking the cake and walked away with the round cake and Hanae. Professor Kozuka was left alone, quietly eating the cake. Professor Kozuka was a tall woman with very short 